  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA broad upper level trough remains over the region today with a\nclosed low off to our north that will slowly drift east into \nwestern Quebec. A potent shortwave embedded in the flow will \nswing through the area early this morning and help drawn in some\nnoticeably cooler air. As this feature moves through, some \nlight showers are expected to accompany it prior to daybreak. \n\nSouthwesterly flow will become more westerly this afternoon, \nand northwesterly into Tuesday, tapping into lake moisture. With\ncool air aloft this afternoon, steepening low-level lapse \nrates, and up to a few hundred J/kg of MUCAPE, scattered showers\nare expected to develop, most widespread from the Twin Tiers on\nnorth. Some small hail can't be ruled out in the heaviest of \nshowers. \n\nLake effect rain showers are expected to become more \nconcentrated north of the Southern Tier tonight before \nnorthwesterly flow Tuesday and lake enhancement with another \nupper level shortwave rotating through along the base of the \ndeparting upper trough spreads showers farther south and east \nonce again. PoPs were increased from the NBM across the Twin \nTiers both this afternoon and Tuesday afternoon as the NBM tends\nto be underdone with shower coverage and not far enough SE in \nthis set up. \n\nIt will be quite cool today with highs in the mid 60s to the \nlower 70s. Portions of SW Steuben County may even struggle to \nreach the lower 60s. Lows tonight will be mainly in the upper \n40s to the mid 50s. Highs Tuesday again generally range from the\nmid 60s to the low 70s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure briefly builds in Tuesday night into Wednesday \nwith dry conditions. The next cold frontal boundary starts to \napproach from the west Thursday and moves through Thursday night\ninto early Friday with scattered showers and thunderstorms. An \narea of low pressure may form near the Southeast Coast and lift \nnorth ahead of the front and enhance the moisture across the \narea which could result in some localized downpours. WPC does \nhas NE PA and the southern Catskills outlooked in a Marginal \nRisk (level 1 out of 4) for excessive rainfall this far out for \nThursday/Thursday night, so this will be a trend to monitor. \nHigh pressure looks to build back in with drier conditions for \nthe start of next weekend. \n\nTemperatures will be on the rise Wednesday through Friday with \nhighs generally in the mid 70s to near 80 degrees. The Lake \nPlain and portions of the Finger Lakes do look a bit warmer on \nThursday ahead of the front with some places reaching the low \nand mid 80s.",
